"""Task-specific interpretation tied to the completed experimental measurements."""
DISCUSSION={
'motion_direction': 'The large increase in direction decodability between appearance and fusion places an accessible direction signal upstream of the recurrent decision states. The neural-state resets produced little impairment at the native displacement strengths, and the long-window comparison state was zero. Together, these observations favor a computation in which the short-lag correspondence representation already supplies sufficient directional evidence for the downstream classifier. The correspondence bank itself uses preceding pixels; resetting a recurrent neural state leaves that causal visual history available. The weak-displacement curve supplies the more sensitive test of the front end than native ceiling performance. A spatial redistribution can also leave many informative dots available, explaining why strong direction information and small quadrant-perturbation effects can coexist.',
'orientation': 'Orientation ordering becomes much more readable in recurrent states than in the capacity-matched appearance and fused representations. Resetting the global reference-period state produced a substantial subsequent error increase, with an additional cost from resetting spatial memory. This pattern links the final response to information carried across the two observations. Concentrating priority in a fixed quadrant also impaired performance, even though the grating occupies the full image. The relevant spatial question is therefore how the selected sample supports a stable comparison, rather than whether an isolated target object receives more attention. The combination of perturbation and decoding localizes a comparison computation more strongly than final CLS decodability alone.',
'contrast': 'The fused representation already carries appreciable information about the correct interval, and recurrent populations make that information nearly fully readable. The largest reference-period memory-reset cost came from spatial memory. This connects the accurate final comparison to a retained spatial description of the first interval. Absolute contrast and the temporal image representation can each provide partial cues; the high terminal accuracy and reset effects reveal how those signals are combined in the trained observer. The subthreshold increment sweep measures the limit of that computation, while quadrant interventions test how concentrated sampling changes the reliability of the evidence being compared.',
'spatial_frequency': 'The answer is most clearly expressed in global recurrent memory, while both global and spatial reference-period resets impair the subsequent judgement. The results support a sequential representation of interval ordering rather than a direct interpretation of a single local edge detector. Spatial priority concentration can additionally reduce performance, consistent with the decision using evidence distributed over the grating. The octave axis makes the behavioral curve a test of relative frequency discrimination. Extending the pedestal range would determine whether the comparison remains accurate when the same relative difference is embedded in unfamiliar absolute frequencies.',
'chromatic_increment': 'Accurate chromatic comparison survives in an architecture whose motion correspondence calculation uses grayscale input. The measured behavior is therefore supported by the color-bearing appearance and local-statistic channels together with recurrent processing. Both global and spatial reference-period resets have substantial effects, and the final answer is strongly readable in those recurrent populations. This provides a static-feature case for a shared spatial-selection architecture: the same priority distribution participates in a computation whose defining information is chromatic. A small effect of one quadrant manipulation can coexist with strong memory dependence when useful color evidence is present throughout the field.',
'contour': 'Contour decisions are almost perfect at the native settings, yet they are vulnerable to resetting either reference-period recurrent state and to concentrating priority in a fixed quadrant. The capacity-matched visual probes are weak compared with the final recurrent readout. This combination is consistent with a distributed configuration judgement assembled through the recurrent pathway. A contour spans multiple elements, so selecting a smaller region can remove useful relational structure even when the local orientation distribution remains informative. The current result establishes sensitivity to the native contour arrangement; independent manipulations of length and curvature would locate the spatial integration limit more precisely.',
'natural_spectrum': 'Spatial-reference memory is a stronger causal bottleneck than the global reference-period state in this experiment, although the final answer is most explicit in global memory. This illustrates a distinction between a representation used to preserve comparison information and the later population from which the decision is read. The natural-image pairs preserve a common mean and RMS contrast while altering spectral detail, so the measured discrimination concerns their structured image content. The example-specific attention overlays identify which image regions receive weight on individual trials, while the separately averaged maps reveal any repeatable spatial preference across photographs.',
'orientation_ring': 'The visible ring supplies a location instruction, and target inhibition at the comparison stage impairs the sign judgement. Resetting either recurrent state immediately before the probe also causes a substantial loss. Final answer information is especially explicit in global memory, while a fixed-capacity spatial probe reads the answer poorly. These findings are compatible with spatial memory retaining task-relevant content that is transformed into an explicit response only when it is selected and compared. The full-state probe control is important here because a small projected subspace need not preserve every feature that the original nonlinear readout can use.',
'orientation_cued': 'The continuous-feature analysis and the response-label analysis describe different stages of the task. Sample orientation remains readable during the delay, whereas the final answer depends on the later rotation and cue rule. A mid-delay spatial reset causes a large subsequent accuracy loss, while the same-time global reset produces no observed loss in this sample. The latter state can be reconstructed during the remaining frames from information still available elsewhere. Target inhibition at the probe separately demonstrates a causal selection requirement. The combined result supports a sequence of retained spatial content, later selection, and rule-dependent response construction.',
'motion_duration_cued': 'The final visual input need not display the accumulated winning direction, yet that direction is strongly readable in recurrent memory. Resetting global or spatial memory during retention impairs the report, and reducing target priority during the moving evidence produces a particularly large cost. This connects performance to both selecting the correct stream and preserving a summary of its temporal history. The comparison-state decoder also contains answer information, but a single reset of that state had little effect at the selected instant. The measured dependencies therefore favor recurrent integration as a major contributor to the duration judgement, while leaving the temporal front end available throughout the movie.',
'krauzlis_cued_motion': 'The target and foil interventions reveal a selection-dependent detector. Suppressing target priority reduces correct reports, and increasing foil priority also reduces performance. The baseline experiment provides little evidence for an additional benefit from foil suppression at the tested native signal strength. Spatial-memory disruption immediately before the change is especially costly, linking the event decision to the preceding cued state. Both global and comparison memories contain readable event information at the end of the trial. A pre-change comparison-memory reset tests dependence at that moment; subsequent visual comparisons remain available and can rebuild later evidence. Event-separated response curves distinguish missed relevant changes from inappropriate foil reports.',
'spatial_binding': 'The all-location reconstruction provides a direct storage result: the original orientation inventory remains readable during the blank interval, before the retrospective cue identifies a target. Spatial-memory reconstruction is particularly strong. A mid-delay spatial reset and a later target-priority suppression each impair the final binding judgement, identifying storage and retrieval selection as separate causal stages. The final answer becomes explicit in global memory after the cue and probe supply the information needed to define it. The preserved inventory has native structure—four equally spaced orientations assigned to locations—so the result concerns retention of that structured array and its spatial assignment.',
'image_recognition': 'Increasing the study load primarily increases false recognition of novel probes, while genuine repeats retain a high hit rate. This separates the dominant error pattern from simple disappearance of every stored image. The spatial-memory reset produces a large cost in the lower-load neural condition, and the final global state carries a strong recognition signal. The serial-position curves describe which stored items are most likely to be accepted; the last-image reconstruction describes only one component of the retained content. Together, these measures support studying familiarity accumulation and interference, alongside the precision with which individual study items can be recovered.',
'vda4': 'The strong-change counterfactual reveals a pronounced cost of removing priority from the cued location and a large cost of increasing that priority when the change is elsewhere. Uncued detection is already high in the 18-degree sham condition. The exploratory two-, four- and eight-degree follow-up removes that ceiling, but still finds no uncued improvement under cue inhibition; at eight degrees, cued detection falls by about 29 percentage points while uncued detection is unchanged. Thus the requested combination of cued cost and uncued benefit is not recovered for this checkpoint in these experiments. Catch trials show how priority also changes positive-response tendency. Native performance at the smallest angle is substantially poorer than at larger angles, making a single pooled score an incomplete description. Because comparison memory remains zero throughout these seven frames, the measured competition is implemented by the shared visual-attention and recurrent pathways.',
'vda9': 'The corner-to-corner counterfactual again shows a strong cued detection cost under cue inhibition. The uncued benefit at the strong signal is smaller and uncertain. The weak-change follow-up again produces a cued cost at eight degrees, about 35 percentage points, with no uncued benefit. It therefore does not recover the joint dual effect after correction across the three VDA follow-ups. Nine stimulus cells occupy unequal numbers of the ten-by-ten model sites, so the recorded mass and affected patch counts help interpret a common per-patch logit dose. Decodable change information increases across the visual-to-recurrent hierarchy, and spatial-memory reset impairs the native condition. The combined measurements connect spatial competition to retained sample information while separating local intervention geometry from behavioral sensitivity.',
'vda16': 'The sixteen-location condition combines greater competition with a different sampling of each Gabor on the common grid. Its weaker native discrimination and the absence of a clear uncued gain in the strong-signal cue-inhibition experiment make it especially useful for separating attention redistribution from successful change extraction. Cue inhibition also changes catch performance, so the report criterion must be measured alongside hits. In the weaker-signal follow-up, cued costs at four and eight degrees survive the cross-VDA correction, while the uncued point estimates remain negative and do not show a reliable improvement. The requested dual effect is therefore absent at these tested strengths for this checkpoint. The recurrent populations contain more answer information than the early capacity-matched visual probes, and spatial-memory reset is harmful. These observations identify recurrent processing as useful even when the final detector remains imperfect.',
'memory_guided_search': 'The family-resolved results expose a selective failure that a task-wide average conceals. Distinct feature objects are matched almost perfectly, feature conjunctions remain substantially above chance, and irregular contour objects are much harder. The matched timing contrasts found no reliable rescue after correction across the tested timing comparisons. Full-resolution reconstruction provides a more specific explanation: spatial memory retains enough object information for an external pixel-distance matcher to achieve 95--100 percent accuracy in the two object conditions, whereas the agent makes much poorer choices on the same scenes. A follow-up also reconstructs the current candidates from local appearance features. Combining those decoded candidates with decoded spatial memory retains 100 percent accuracy for distinct objects and about 93 percent for similar objects, localizing a substantial deficit to the use of available representations. Joint decoding also improves distinct and similar fractals, although similar-fractal accuracy remains about 52 percent. Probe pairs are fitted separately within each stimulus condition, so these comparisons establish condition-specific information availability. Thus errors in using stored content and limitations of the stored or decoded representation coexist across stimulus families. The direct map check adds a second distinction: target stimulation raises final object-target allocation from about 21 percent to about 89 percent on the replay subset, while the full paired object experiments show only a partial behavioral rescue. Correct spatial allocation alone therefore does not restore successful object comparison. The changed-sample, identical-array experiment independently measures whether the agent uses its remembered sample to control choice and spatial allocation. Together, these measurements motivate a direct route from distributed spatial memory into the search computation, while identifying similar fractals as a separate demanding test of representational precision.',
'popout_search': 'Additional processing of the same static array produces a large behavioral benefit. At the smallest color difference, accuracy rises from about 48 percent after one array frame to 100 percent after two. At a five-degree orientation difference it rises from 25 percent to about 83 percent after two frames, reaching about 86 percent after eight. The matched one-versus-eight-frame comparisons survive correction in five of the six feature-difference groups; the largest color difference is already at ceiling. Thus a physically available singleton is not necessarily converted into a correct location report on the first processing step. Repeated frames permit both recurrent state updates and re-evaluation of the visual pathways. The priority and state interventions then ask which parts of that processing support selection. Since item count is fixed at four, these curves characterize feature discrimination and temporal processing within that array size.',
'motion_memory_search': 'Across 81 native conditions, mean accuracy is about 97.7 percent. The paired timing contrasts show no corrected benefit from extending the tested sample or array durations, consistent with high performance at the shorter settings. The sample and search candidates use independent dot layouts, so the task requires a motion template that survives a change in visual realization. At the middle delay, a capacity-matched linear decoder reconstructs the remembered direction with R-squared about 0.76 from global memory and 0.87 from spatial memory. Resetting global memory there reduces accuracy by about 73 percentage points in both selected direction-spacing conditions, whereas the spatial reset is less damaging. Target-priority inhibition during search separately costs about 70 to 90 percentage points. This combination identifies readable retained content, strong dependence on global memory, and a causal selection requirement in a successful search computation. Continuous direction reconstruction tests retention of that template, while the final location decoder tests its use after the candidates appear. Matched timing effects separate sample evidence from search evidence and intervening retention. The identical-array query switch provides a direct causal test of template dependence: only the preceding sample direction changes. Comparing its behavioral probability contrast with its attention contrast reveals whether memory influences spatial selection, the downstream decision, or both.'
}
